        {
          "id": 1913546,
          "username": "Maciej_Lektoro",
          "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/community.shopify.com/maciej_lektoro/{size}/416951_2.png",
          "created_at": "2026-10-13T23:22:27.452Z",
          "cooked": "<p>From the implementation side, I’d make the review screen show both the quantity on the incoming order and the quantity that will go into Shopify. For example, <strong>2 cases × 12 units = 24 units</strong>. Keeping “cases” as an explicit field avoids turning that into an order for two individual items.</p>\n<p>I’d also flag a SKU that matches more than one variant, instead of choosing the first match. And if the same customer sends the same PO reference again, show the existing draft and let someone decide whether it’s a resend or a revision. That’s a useful check before automating email/PDF intake.</p>\n<p>One Shopify detail: saving a draft and holding the stock are separate decisions. If those units need to be held while the stockist reviews the invoice, use <strong>Reserve inventory</strong> with an expiry; otherwise they remain available to other customers. <a href=\"https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/fulfillment/managing-orders/create-orders/create-draft\">Shopify’s draft-order guide</a>.</p>\n<p>Are you seeing more unit/case mistakes, or repeated and amended orders?</p>",
