    "ai_summary": {
      "id": 1836322,
      "summarized_text": "The discussion examines how smaller wholesale brands on standard Shopify plans enter stockist orders without a full B2B portal. The original question asks about order volumes, staff time, common errors, and tools tried, but no one provides order counts or time estimates, so those comparisons remain unanswered.\n\nPractical suggestions focus on standardizing order information before automating intake. A form or spreadsheet can capture SKUs, quantities, case packs, wholesale prices, and PO numbers; staff can then create Shopify draft orders and review exceptions. Draft orders allow custom line prices, SKU search, and PO details in notes or attributes, while existing drafts can be duplicated for repeat customers.\n\nSeveral safeguards are recommended: represent cases as distinct variants with their own SKUs, clearly show case-to-unit conversions, flag ambiguous SKU matches, and check for duplicate or revised PO references. Drafting an order does not reserve inventory; merchants must separately use Shopify’s inventory-reservation option if stock needs to be held. No shared workflow or final decision emerges, and the discussion remains open for actual merchant experiences.",
